  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure is settled in at the surface and keeping our \natmosphere capped along with plenty of dry air in the column \nalong with modest southerly flow. \n\nThis will prevent any precipitation through the rest of the \nwork week as this dominating surface high pressure system will \nkeep us shielded for now and moderate temperatures back to \nabove seasonable. \n\nThis will subsequently keep overnight lows warmer to where the \narea does not have to worry about frost or freeze this week or \nthe weekend. \n\nUpper level ridging reinforces the fair weather out to at least \nthis weekend or until Sunday. \n\nA long stretch of dry weather comes to an end courtesy of a \nsouthern stream system forecast to impede the area on Sunday. \n\nChances for precipitation will spread across the entire area for\na chance to get some impactful rain whether a wetting rain to \nmitigate potential fire concerns or an abundance of rain \npossibly raising some isolated flash flooding concerns only \nslightly.\n\nAnomalously high PWATS for this time of year come into fruition \nby the impact timing of the aforementioned system. This system is\nforecast to take a northeasterly track up through the area and \nprovided plenty of moisture to the area, however impacts are\nlimited as of now.\n\nMainly thinking about flood prone or very low lying areas with \npoor drainage are of concern if values of precipitation increase\nacross the area as we get closer to the time frame. \n\nModels depart significantly on tracking and timing. As of now, \nnothing to worry about, but worth keeping on eye on and to see \nwhere the highest amounts of rainfall will be forecast.",
